> Distance identity and its scope

Keeping a common subsequence of length L deletes n−L elements from a and inserts
m−L from b, costing n+m−2L. Conversely, the undeleted elements of any
insertion/deletion edit script form a common subsequence, so no cheaper script
exists. This identity concerns insertions/deletions only. A substitution with
unit cost can beat one deletion plus one insertion: the one-element sequences1
and2 have LCS length0 and insertion/deletion distance2, but unit-substitution
Levenshtein distance1. No report code implements another edit-distance
algorithm.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lcs_distance.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| original first sequence length | original second sequence length | actual LCS length | " "derived insertion/deletion distance |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> b{{1, 2, 3}, {1, 3}, {3, 2, 1}, {4, 5, 6}, {}};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < b.size(); ++i) {
        auto a = dm::algorithms::lcs_analyze({1, 2, 3}, b[i]);
        auto d = 3 + b[i].size() - 2 * a.sequence.size();
        std::print("| 3 | {} | {} | {} |\n", b[i].size(), a.sequence.size(), d);
        x.push_back(i);
        y.push_back(d);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "derived exact distance", {37, 99, 235}, 6).y_limits(-.5, 8).title("Insertion/deletion distances from actual optimal lengths").x_label("listed sequence-pair index").y_label("n plus m minus twice LCS length").save_svg("build/lcs_distance.svg");
}
```
