| 64 | 4096 | 64 | 62 |
| 128 | 16384 | 128 | 126 |
| 256 | 65536 | 256 | 254 |
\image(figures/lcs_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual DP and traceback counts on explicit deterministic sequences.)
\alttext(Separate panels avoid concealing traceback work beneath the quadratic cell scale; these counts exclude allocation, copying and elapsed time.)

> Contracts and verification

Empty sequences and arbitrary int values are valid. Complete table/container
geometry, possible int-length overflow and move-count geometry are checked
before allocation. Capture conservatively admits n+m+(n+1)*(m+1)+n+m logical
records before evaluation, including an upper bound on moves; captured_entries
subsequently reports the actual input/table/move count. Thus a quota can reject
a run whose eventual actual move count would fit. The default cap is one million
records, zero disables only that optional cap, and
fields/capacities/output/runtime copies and bytes are excluded. All
captures/results own their data.

Independent exhaustive subsequence enumeration verified500 integer-sequence
pairs and actual optimal lengths/results. Another1000 selected outputs matched
the frozen tie convention exactly. Native tests exhaust3969 binary-word pairs,
check every actual table recurrence and chosen move, preserve the original BCAB
selection and cover empty/extreme-int/quota/ownership controls. The complete
ten-report batch passed77 native CTest cases,39 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan
cases,106 compiled/executed public examples,479/479 scoped definitions and ten
representative clangd hovers. All4 complete report programs executed and all4
native figures were visually reviewed. HTML/Markdown computed result and
identical source/build/export figure bytes passed verification. Repeatable
receipts are retained under `build/mepml-conversion/batch-194-203/`.

> References

D. S. Hirschberg(1975),
[A Linear Space Algorithm for Computing Maximal Common Subsequences|https://doi.org/10.1145/360825.360861],
Communications of the ACM18(6),341–343. This report explicitly uses the
full-table baseline, retaining its actual table for inspection rather than
implementing the paper’s linear-space construction.
